  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Nobody ever found the manhole cover a nuclear test launched. Nevada, August 27th, 1957. A two-thousand-pound steel lid, welded over a borehole.",
        "visual_query": "nevada desert test site 1950s",
        "visual_fallback": "desert flats"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Underneath sat a bomb meant to barely fizzle. It yielded about three hundred tons instead. The shaft became a gun barrel.",
        "visual_query": "1950s atomic test desert flash",
        "visual_fallback": "atomic test"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A camera shooting a thousand frames a second caught the lid in exactly one frame. One. So how fast was it moving?",
        "visual_query": "vintage high speed film camera",
        "visual_fallback": "film camera"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Los Alamos physicist Robert Brownlee ran the numbers and got six times Earth's escape velocity. He never recovered the cap.",
        "visual_query": "1950s physicist blackboard calculations",
        "visual_fallback": "chalkboard equations"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It almost certainly never reached space. Compression heating would have vaporised the steel on the way up, which means the fastest thing humans had ever made burned itself out of existence.",
        "visual_query": "desert sky above nevada flats",
        "visual_fallback": "desert sky"
      }
    ],
